WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.557
Here we create a simple teaching example of a customer quality score.

00:00:04.557 --> 00:00:11.792
The query checks four conditions: whether the name is present, whether the email has a valid shape,

00:00:11.792 --> 00:00:18.262
whether the phone has ten digits, and whether the signup date passes the selected date rule.

00:00:18.262 --> 00:00:25.795
Each condition contributes twenty-five points, so a record can receive a score from zero to one hundred.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:32.914
The mathematics is simple, but the important question is why each check receives the same weight.

00:00:32.914 --> 00:00:38.859
In a real organization, perhaps a valid email is more important than a phone number.

00:00:38.859 --> 00:00:43.189
Or perhaps the signup date should not affect quality at all.

00:00:43.189 --> 00:00:48.434
So treat this score as an illustration, not a universal formula.

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:51.704
A composite score is a policy decision.

00:00:51.704 --> 00:00:58.395
Always keep the underlying component checks available so people can understand what the final number means.
